WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.969 --> 00:00:06.580
Most, if not all, programming languages have
built-in libraries of code that focus on specific

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:14.500
areas for built-in functions, such as system
operations, file operations, and math functions.

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:17.540
Python refers to these libraries as modules.

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:22.480
As you are about to see, these modules must
be imported before they can be used.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:24.750
Modules are often called libraries.

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:26.540
They don't just offer convenience.

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:34.620
They help in organizing code, enhancing reusability,
and separating concerns within a program.

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:39.309
The first such module is the io module, short
for input/output.

00:00:39.309 --> 00:00:44.489
io allows one to store data and then read
it from memory instead of creating and working

00:00:44.489 --> 00:00:48.530
with a physical file, which we did elsewhere
in the course.

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:53.039
If game-playing data needs to be stored and
retrieved during a game and not between game

00:00:53.039 --> 00:00:58.329
sessions, there is no need to store the data
on a physical file and risk the data being

00:00:58.329 --> 00:00:59.660
hacked.

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:05.070
The code in this 611-io file stores data and
then retrieves it without using a physical

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:06.620
file.

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:12.170
On line 1, we import the io library, which
gives us access to many built-in io classes

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:13.909
and functions.

00:01:13.909 --> 00:01:19.869
One such class is StringIO, which allows one
to input text strings into memory and retrieve

00:01:19.869 --> 00:01:23.119
them later, much like blobs in JavaScript.

00:01:23.119 --> 00:01:29.780
On line 3, I have a StringIO instance assigned
to a game stream variable.

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:36.090
Lines 5 and 6 have write commands to write
information to the game stream.

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:44.380
Line 8 runs the seek function, which seeks
stored text at a given character, character

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:48.950
0 being the first character in the text.

00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:53.880
On line 9, we have a print statement that
prints the reading of the text.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:02:01.180
I will run the code, and we see the output
in the terminal like we did for working with

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:03.380
text files.

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:09.720
However, using this input output functionality,
there are no files to manage on one’s hard

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:10.720
drive.

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:15.440
The takeaway here is that, when storing and
retrieving data, if the storage is temporary,

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:21.050
consider using input output streams instead
of creating and then having to delete physical

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:23.000
files to complete these tasks.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:27.970
Furthermore, the io module's objects are often
compatible with other Python standard library

00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:33.630
modules that expect file-like objects, adding
to their utility.

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:36.239
The io module is not limited to text.

00:02:36.239 --> 00:02:41.830
It also allows for in-memory storage of binary
data using the BytesIO class.

00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:46.940
This comes in handy when you're dealing with
non-text files like images or executables.

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:53.050
Both StringIO and BytesIO create file-like
objects, meaning you can use methods like

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:59.819
.read(), .write(), and .seek() as if you were
interacting with an actual disk-based file.

00:02:59.819 --> 00:03:02.489
However, there are some differences.

00:03:02.489 --> 00:03:07.110
For example, when working with actual files
in Python, you often need to explicitly manage

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:14.049
text encodings like UTF-8, Unicode, and others,
but with StringIO, you don’t have to worry

00:03:14.049 --> 00:03:18.590
about specifying text encoding because the
class handles it for you.

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:24.810
Beyond gaming, consider using io for unit
testing, where you can mimic file operations

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:27.239
without disk-based files.

00:03:27.239 --> 00:03:32.830
It's also useful for quick data transformations
and in-memory caching.

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:37.610
If you’re interested in diving deeper into
the functionalities of the io module, or any

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:45.090
other topics covered in this course, the Python
official documentation is an excellent resource.

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:49.670
You can find comprehensive details, examples,
and best practices there.

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:56.560
Though not on the exam, this docs.python.org
site has valuable documentation on Python

00:03:56.560 --> 00:03:57.650
libraries.

00:03:57.650 --> 00:04:02.329
Here is one such documentation page, the page
for the io library.

